**Diary — 2026-03-18 — Porting the CUDA engine to HIP on gfx906**

Today I moved the core CUDA engine onto HIP for gfx906 (Vega 10 / Radeon VII class). The goal was not a toy demo; I wanted the existing kernel pipeline to run on AMD with the same numerical output and a predictable performance envelope.

The first concrete change was the build. I replaced the CUDA toolchain with ROCm/HIP and compiled with:

```bash
hipcc -O3 -std=c++17 \
  --offload-arch=gfx906 \
  -D__HIP_PLATFORM_AMD__ \
  -Iinclude \
  src/engine.cu \
  src/kernels.cu \
  -o engine_hip
```

I kept the CUDA-style source for now and used `hipify-perl` as a first pass, then manually cleaned the output. The automatic conversion got most of the obvious things:

```cpp
#include <hip/hip_runtime.h>

__global__ void reduce_kernel(float* out, const float* in, int n) {
    __shared__ float smem[256];

    int tid = threadIdx.x;
    int idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;

    float v = (idx < n) ? in[idx] : 0.0f;

    smem[tid] = v;
    __syncthreads();

    for (int s = blockDim.x / 2; s > 0; s >>= 1) {
        if (tid < s) {
            smem[tid] += smem[tid + s];
        }
        __syncthreads();
    }

    if (tid == 0) {
        atomicAdd(out, smem[0]);
    }
}
```

That compiled, but the first real gfx906-specific issue was the wavefront size. CUDA assumes 32-thread warps. gfx906 uses 64-thread wavefronts. The CUDA shuffle code:

```cpp
float x = __shfl_down_sync(0xffffffff, v, 16);
```

